[48;2;47;48;54m [38;2;117;121;127mmain[39m [38;2;197;201;199m~/repos/pi-durable-lab[39m                                                                                                                                                                [38;2;117;121;127m[Dashboard][39m
    [38;2;197;201;199monly read and bash.[39m
    [38;2;117;121;127m2. [1m[38;2;197;201;199mA scheduled ping from outside.[0m[38;2;197;201;199m[48;2;47;48;54m launchd on the mini runs [1mdpi ask "<heartbeat prompt>" <session>[0m[38;2;197;201;199m[48;2;47;48;54m on a schedule. It needs no code, so it can run today. The catch is that every wake adds to one[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mlong session's context, so the session needs compaction or a fresh one every so often.[39m
    [38;2;117;121;127m3. [1m[38;2;197;201;199mWake on change.[0m[38;2;197;201;199m[48;2;47;48;54m Wake the agent only when something it watches changes, such as a file, folder or repo. change-watch already detects file changes; you'd add an agent turn when it finds one.[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mOf the three, this burns the least credit.[39m

    [38;2;197;201;199mFor the credit deadline, the useful split is:[39m

    [38;2;117;121;127m• [1m[38;2;197;201;199mA fixed-time heartbeat:[0m[38;2;197;201;199m[48;2;47;48;54m cheap and predictable, but it mostly spends on "nothing to do".[39m
    [38;2;117;121;127m• [1m[38;2;197;201;199mLong tasks:[0m[38;2;197;201;199m[48;2;47;48;54m a long research or build task actually turns the $101 into work.[39m

    [38;2;197;201;199mSo I'd start with option 2 for the heartbeat today and put most of the API credit on two or three long tasks.[39m

    [38;2;197;201;199mWhich domains do you want first? That decides which mechanism each one gets.[39m

 [48;2;67;70;78m   [38;2;197;201;199mInvesting in this infrastructure and creating "durable" pun intended agents is probably wise[39m                                                                                                       [48;2;47;48;54m
 [48;2;67;70;78m                                                                                                                                                                                                      [48;2;47;48;54m
 [48;2;67;70;78m   [38;2;197;201;199mSo domains that come to mind …[39m                                                                                                                                                                     [48;2;47;48;54m

